"<strong>Setups in the demo</strong> (the Presets chips that start with Liquid)<strong>:</strong> Liquid: Faraday squares (water {#} mm deep at {#} Hz, {#} times the onset); Liquid: stripes in thick oil (silicone oil, {#} cSt, {#} mm deep at {#} Hz); Liquid: hexagons (silicone oil, {#} cSt, {#} mm deep at {#} Hz); Liquid: eightfold pattern (silicone oil, {#} cSt, {#} mm deep at {#} Hz); Liquid: find the onset (water {#} mm deep in a square dish at {#} Hz, driven at {#} of the onset). The demo opens on a metal plate; any Liquid preset, or <strong>Vibrating: Liquid layer</strong>, puts a dish of liquid on the wave driver.",
"The picture is the liquid surface seen from above, lit from the side, with the dish’s motion and the surface’s own motion slowed by the factor f so you can follow them; the waves grow or die away in real time. The plot beside it (the Display fold, <strong>Plot</strong>) shows <strong>Onset</strong>, the dish acceleration Γ needed for waves at every drive frequency, or <strong>Tongues</strong>, which wavelengths grow at each Γ.",
"The function generator sets <strong>Frequency f</strong> ({#} to {#} Hz for a liquid) with its knobs and sliders; click the frequency readout to type an exact value. Set up the plate and driver: <strong>Vibrating</strong> (Metal plate, Drumhead, Liquid layer), <strong>Shape</strong> of the dish, <strong>Liquid</strong> (Water, Silicone oil, {#} cSt, Silicone oil, {#} cSt), <strong>Liquid depth d</strong> from {#} to {#} mm in {#} mm steps, and <strong>Driver setting x₀</strong>, which sets how hard the driver shakes the dish.",
"Readouts: under Drive and surface, <strong>Dish acceleration Γ = a/g</strong>, <strong>Onset Γ at this f</strong>, the dish motion, the driver force and <strong>Surface</strong>, which says whether waves grow (and how fast, with their frequency, wavelength and pattern) or how far below onset you are; under Liquid, the liquid mass, <strong>Free wave at f/{#}</strong> (its frequency and wavelength) and <strong>Damping and pattern</strong>.",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> stores the dish shape, the liquid, its depth, f, Γ, the onset Γ, the surface (flat, or the pattern), the surface frequency, the wavelength of the growing wave and that of the free wave. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> fills the Data table in order: Reading (with the dish shape), Liquid, Depth, Drive f, Γ, Onset Γ_c, Surface, Surface frequency and Wavelength (the free wave’s); the last three columns are yours to work out.",
"The dish rides on the driver as one rigid load. In the dish’s frame gravity becomes \\(g(1 + \\Gamma\\cos 2\\pi f t)\\), so each surface wave of wavenumber \\(k\\) obeys a damped Mathieu equation, \\(\\ddot a + 2\\alpha\\dot a + \\left[\\omega_0^2 + \\Gamma g k\\tanh(kd)\\cos 2\\pi f t\\right]a = 0,\\) with \\(\\omega_0^2 = (gk + \\sigma k^3/\\rho)\\tanh(kd)\\). The demo integrates it over one drive period (fourth order Runge–Kutta) and finds whether the wave grows from the Floquet multipliers. The onset \\(\\Gamma_c\\) is the least Γ at which any wave grows.",
"Damping: the bulk viscous rate \\(2\\nu k^2\\) plus the boundary layer on the dish floor, \\(k\\sqrt{\\nu\\omega/2}/\\sinh 2kd\\). The side walls, the meniscus and any film on the surface are left out; they all add damping, so measured onsets in water are often several times the demo’s.",
"Which pattern forms is a nonlinear question the linear model cannot answer. The demo follows a rule taken from experiments (Binks and van de Water {#}; Kudrolli and Gollub {#}; Chen and Viñals {#}), given in Background. The height of the pattern is a picture, not a calculation, and droplets thrown off well above onset are not modeled.",
"Liquids: water at {#} °C, {#} kg/m³, {#} mPa·s (CRC Handbook), \\(\\sigma\\) = {#} mN/m (IAPWS); silicone oils at {#} °C (Dow XIAMETER PMX-{#} data sheet): {#} cSt, {#} kg/m³, {#} mN/m; {#} cSt, {#} kg/m³, {#} mN/m. The readings are simulated: model results, not measurements.",
"Find the drive needed to make a shaken liquid ripple, the frequency at which its surface moves, and how the waves’ wavelength depends on frequency; from that dependence measure the surface tension of water and \\(g\\), and see how depth and viscosity raise the onset and choose the pattern.",
"<strong>Waves on a liquid.</strong> A ripple on water is pulled flat by two things: gravity, which pulls the crests down, and surface tension \\(\\sigma\\), which pulls a curved surface flat like a stretched sheet. A wave of wavelength \\(\\lambda\\), wavenumber \\(k = 2\\pi/\\lambda\\), on a layer of depth \\(d\\) has angular frequency \\(\\omega\\) given by",
"\\[\\omega^2 = \\left(gk + \\frac{\\sigma k^3}{\\rho}\\right)\\tanh(kd).\\]",
"When the layer is deeper than about two thirds of a wavelength (\\(kd\\) above {#}), \\(\\tanh(kd)\\) is {#} to better than {#} %, and dividing by \\(k\\) gives a straight line in \\(k^2\\):",
"\\[\\frac{\\omega^2}{k} = g + \\frac{\\sigma}{\\rho}\\,k^2 .\\]",
"Long waves are held flat mostly by gravity, short ones mostly by surface tension.",
"<strong>Shaking the dish.</strong> Shake a dish of liquid up and down at frequency \\(f\\) with acceleration amplitude \\(a\\), and write \\(\\Gamma = a/g\\). Seen from the dish, gravity swings between \\(g(1 - \\Gamma)\\) and \\(g(1 + \\Gamma)\\), \\(f\\) times a second. The surface is never pushed sideways, yet above a threshold it breaks into standing waves: Faraday waves (Michael Faraday, {#}). The drive does not push the waves; it changes how strongly they are pulled back. This is parametric drive, the way a child pumps a swing by standing and squatting: standing at each end of the swing and squatting at the bottom pumps twice per swing. In the same way a surface wave is pumped hardest when gravity goes through two cycles while the wave goes through one, so the waves that grow first have angular frequency",
"\\[\\omega_0 = \\tfrac12\\,(2\\pi f) = \\pi f,\\]",
"half the drive frequency (a subharmonic response). Their wavelength is the one the formula above gives for that \\(\\omega\\).",
"<strong>Damping and the onset.</strong> A wave loses energy to viscosity at the rate \\(2\\alpha\\), where the amplitude decay rate of a wave on a deep layer is \\(\\alpha = 2\\nu k^2\\), with \\(\\nu\\) the kinematic viscosity (water, {#} mm²/s). It grows only when the pumping beats that loss. For weak damping (\\(\\alpha\\) much less than \\(\\omega_0\\)) and a deep layer, the onset is",
"\\[\\Gamma_c \\approx \\frac{4\\alpha}{\\omega_0}\\left(1 + \\frac{\\sigma k^2}{\\rho g}\\right).\\]",
"A shallow layer adds a second loss, the drag of the dish floor on the moving liquid, so its onset is higher.",
"<strong>Patterns.</strong> Just above onset the surface picks a pattern. Experiments find a rule, which the demo follows: stripes when damping is strong (\\(\\alpha/\\omega_0\\) above about {#}); otherwise squares when surface tension supplies most of the pull (the capillary share \\(s = \\sigma k^2/(\\rho g + \\sigma k^2)\\) at least {#}), hexagons for \\(s\\) from {#} to {#}, and an eightfold quasipattern, which never repeats, below {#}.",
"The dish is shaken up and down {#} times a second. Before you open the demo, predict how many times a second a point on the rippled surface rises and falls: {#}, {#} or {#}. Give your reason.",
"For water (\\(\\rho\\) = {#} kg/m³, \\(\\sigma\\) = {#} mN/m), at what wavelength do the two terms of \\(g + (\\sigma/\\rho)k^2\\) equal each other? Are the ripples in a dish shaken at {#} Hz (wavelength about {#} mm) held flat more by gravity or by surface tension?",
